    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d12">

                <pb facs="0056" n="18" sameAs="frus:frus1955-57v27/pg18"/>

                <head>12. Telegram From the Embassy in France to the Department of State<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d12-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                ><placeName>Paris</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                                when="1955-12-08T20:00:00+01:00">December 8, 1955— 8
                            p.m.</date></hi></dateline></opener>
                <p>2762. Margerie has shown us December 7 Reuter despatch from Washington saying
                    Washington officials believe Eden visit<anchor n="2" xml:id="d12-f2"/> marks
                    approaching end of postwar big-three meetings, that German and Italian claims to
                    be heard on world problems now rival those of <pb facs="0057" n="19"
                        xml:id="pg19"/> France, that five power talks would be too big, and that
                    hereafter U.S. will concentrate both on NATO political discussions and on
                    bilateral talks. While despatch mentioned U.S. desire for visit from next French
                    Prime Minister, leftist press, notably <hi rendition="#italic">Express</hi> and
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Combat</hi> slur over this but feature balance of
                    despatch as indicating eviction of French from big three.</p>
                <p>Margerie states that Pinay fully realizes despatch is malicious Reuter distortion
                    of U.S. thinking but that he is nevertheless seriously disturbed by use which
                    Mendes as well as other opponents of government may make of it in electoral
                    campaign. He would therefore be most grateful if Secretary would utilize
                    occasion of his departure for Paris<anchor n="3" xml:id="d12-f3"/> or earlier
                    occasion to state that he was looking forward to discussing matters of common
                    interest with Pinay and that he would be discussing with Pinay and
                        Macmillan<anchor n="4" xml:id="d12-f4"/> general international situation
                    following Geneva<anchor n="5" xml:id="d12-f5"/> and perhaps such other questions
                    as Middle East.<anchor n="6" xml:id="d12-f6"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Dillon</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d12-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d12-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 611.51/12–855. Secret; Priority.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d12-f2" n="2" xml:id="d12-f2-content">
                <p>Sir Anthony Eden visited Washington, January 30–February 1, 1956.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d12-f3" n="3" xml:id="d12-f3-content">
                <p>Dulles was in Paris, December 14–18, to attend the meeting of the North Atlantic
                    Council.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d12-f4" n="4" xml:id="d12-f4-content">
                <p>Harold Macmillan also attended the North Atlantic Council meetings.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d12-f5" n="5" xml:id="d12-f5-content">
                <p>Reference is to the meetings at Geneva of the Foreign Ministers of the United
                    States, United Kingdom, France, and the Soviet Union, October 27–November 16,
                    1955; see volume V.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d12-f6" n="6" xml:id="d12-f6-content">
                <p>For text of Dulles’ statement, December 13, see Department of State <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, December 26, 1955, p. 1084.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
